    <code code="11450-4" codeSystem="2.16.840.1.113883.6.1" displayName="Problem List"/>
    <title>Problems</title>
    <text><table><thead><tr><th>Problem</th><th>Clinical status</th><th>Verification</th><th>Code</th><th>Provenance</th></tr></thead><tbody>
  <tr ID="problem-1">
    <td>Thirst, frequent urination day and night, tiredness and about 4 kg weight loss over two months, with a random glucose of 246 mg/dL; told by physicians a year ago that he had diabetes, never confirmed with modern tests or treated except by diet. Diabetes to be confirmed and classified (type 2, type 1 or slowly progressive autoimmune diabetes) with A1c, fasting glucose, GAD antibodies and C-peptide</td><td>resolved</td><td>confirmed</td>
    <td>R73.9</td><td>S — Synthetic presenting problem anchored to his documented diabetes of the year before. Superseded by the diagnosis at the second visit.</td>
  </tr>
  <tr ID="problem-2">
    <td>Type 2 diabetes: A1c 9.4 percent and fasting glucose 172 mg/dL, with symptoms; GAD65 antibodies negative and fasting C-peptide preserved (2.6 ng/mL); no ketones. BMI 24.6, in the range where ADA screens Asian Americans. Classified as type 2, not type 1 or slowly progressive autoimmune diabetes</td><td>active</td><td>confirmed</td>
    <td>E11.65</td><td>S — Synthetic modern confirmation and classification linked to his documented diabetes.</td>
  </tr>
  <tr ID="problem-3">
    <td>Moderately increased albuminuria on a first sample (urine albumin-to-creatinine ratio 46 mg/g), eGFR normal; to be confirmed on two of three samples over three to six months</td><td>active</td><td>confirmed</td>
    <td>R80.9</td><td>S — Synthetic finding linked to his documented albuminuria; superseded when diabetic kidney disease was confirmed.</td>
  </tr>
  <tr ID="problem-4">
    <td>Raised blood pressure in clinic (138/86, average of two readings) without a diagnosis of hypertension; home readings to be taken</td><td>resolved</td><td>confirmed</td>
    <td>R03.0</td><td>S — Synthetic finding; not a claim about his real blood pressure.</td>
  </tr>
  <tr ID="problem-5">
    <td>Hypertension, stage 1: clinic 138/86, 136/86 and 134/84; home average 136/86 over seven days; with moderately increased albuminuria and diabetes</td><td>active</td><td>confirmed</td>
    <td>I10</td><td>S — Synthetic finding of the modern episode; not a claim about his real blood pressure, which was never recorded.</td>
  </tr>
  <tr ID="problem-6">
    <td>Long-standing difficulty falling asleep: by his own account he has never been able to fall asleep on getting into bed and lies awake for two hours or more most nights; worse lately with waking to pass urine; rises before dawn for meditation</td><td>active</td><td>confirmed</td>
    <td>G47.00</td><td>S — Synthetic modern problem linked to his documented lifelong difficulty falling asleep.</td>
  </tr>
  <tr ID="problem-7">
    <td>Mixed hyperlipidemia: LDL cholesterol 132 mg/dL, triglycerides 228, HDL 36</td><td>active</td><td>confirmed</td>
    <td>E78.2</td><td>S — Synthetic cardiovascular risk factor.</td>
  </tr>
  <tr ID="problem-8">
    <td>Attacks of wheeze and breathlessness for three weeks since coming down from the mountains to the damp plains, the worst last night with a sense of suffocation: peak flow 330 L/min (56 percent of predicted), oxygen saturation 94 percent, rising to 410 L/min after bronchodilator; no fever, edema or chest pain; chest X-ray and ECG without heart failure or pneumonia. Asthma to be confirmed with spirometry</td><td>active</td><td>confirmed</td>
    <td>R06.2</td><td>S — Synthetic presenting problem anchored to his documented suffocating attacks of asthma on his return to Belur. Superseded by the diagnosis a week later.</td>
  </tr></tbody></table></text>

