  <section>
    <templateId root="2.16.840.1.113883.10.20.22.2.9" extension="2014-06-09"/>
    <code code="51847-2" codeSystem="2.16.840.1.113883.6.1" displayName="Evaluation + Plan note"/>
    <title>Assessment and Plan</title>
    <text><list listType="ordered"><item><content styleCode="Bold">Attacks of wheeze and breathlessness: probable asthma, moderate attack (R06.2)</content>. A moderate attack of what is most likely asthma, beginning in adult life and brought on by the damp plains and smoke: peak flow 56 percent of predicted rising 24 percent with albuterol, oxygen saturation 94 percent, speaking in sentences. Heart failure and pneumonia are unlikely: no fever, edema or orthopnea, a normal jugular venous pressure, and a chest X-ray without edema or consolidation; the ECG shows only sinus tachycardia. Rapid influenza and COVID-19 tests are negative. Safe to go home with treatment. [S]<list><item>Prednisone 40 mg each morning for five days.</item><item>Budesonide-formoterol one inhalation as needed, up to 12 a day, with a spacer, until spirometry next week.</item><item>Emergency care or 911 for breathlessness at rest, inability to speak in sentences, blue lips or no relief from the inhaler.</item><item>Avoid kitchen smoke and incense in his room for now.</item><item>Review in one week with spirometry before and after bronchodilator and FeNO.</item></list></item><item><content styleCode="Bold">Type 2 diabetes (E11.65)</content>. Prednisone will raise his glucose, mainly in the afternoon and evening, for the five days and a day or two after. He is not fasting this week. [S]<list><item>Check glucose before breakfast and before the evening meal while on prednisone.</item><item>Continue metformin; call if two readings are above 300 mg/dL or he cannot eat or drink, so that short-term insulin can be considered.</item><item>No religious fast while he is on prednisone and unwell, as he agreed.</item></list></item></list><paragraph>Guideline basis: 6. Glycemic Goals, Hypoglycemia, and Hyperglycemic Crises: Standards of Care in Diabetes-2026 (2026); Global Strategy for Asthma Management and Prevention, 2025 update (2025); 2020 Focused Updates to the Asthma Management Guidelines: A Report from the National Asthma Education and Prevention Program Coordinating Committee Expert Panel Working Group (2020)</paragraph><paragraph>Follow-up: Review in one week with spirometry and FeNO; telephone call from the nurse in two days about breathing and glucose. Next scheduled visit 1898-10-27: review after seven months away and a week after an asthma attack: A1c, kidneys, lipids, spirometry with bronchodilator response and FeNO; asthma and diabetic kidney disease confirmed; empagliflozin and maintenance-and-reliever inhaler; fasting plan for the coming festival. [S]</paragraph></text>

  </section>
